N:HOW READ (2026-11-11): No source plate exists because this is a new composition; this is the compositional audit. Every bar totals eight eighths in both parts, with no pickup. Voice: bar 1 G4 and B4 quarters, D5 eighth, B4 quarter on the off-beat, A4 eighth; bar 2 B4 eighth, A4 quarter, G4 eighth (syn-co-pa), half rest; bar 3 accented D5 quarter, C5-B4 eighths, accented D5 quarter, C5-B4 eighths; bar 4 C5-B4-A4 syn-co-pa, G4 half; bar 5 G4 and B4 quarters, D5 eighth, E5 quarter, D5 eighth; bar 6 as bar 2; bar 7 as bar 3; bar 8 B4-A4-B4 syn-co-pa, G4 half. Every syncopated note is a quarter starting on an off-beat eighth and ending on the next off-beat; no dotted notes, sixteenths or ties. Clapped part: B eighth, B quarter, B eighth, quarter rest, B quarter in every bar. Pitch set G-A-B-C-D-E, range G4-E5; within each phrase the line moves by steps and thirds, and the widest move is the fifth from G4 up to D5 across the half rest into each chorus (bars 2-3 and 6-7). Form: call, chorus, varied call, chorus with a new cadence on the tonic. Originality check: compared with "Que Bloco É Esse?" (melody and chorus not used) and with common chants and school songs (We Will Rock You's stomp-stomp-clap, Kye Kye Kule, Che Che Koolay, Hey Ho Nobody Home); no match beyond the common syn-co-pa cell.
N:UNCERTAIN: Whether Grade 4 singers keep the syncopated quarters against the clapped ostinato at 104, and whether the band's drums should be muted by default, can only be settled by a classroom trial. The album pairing (Canto Negro, 1984) comes from a discography listing that was not opened directly; the history and the founders' names need an Afro-Brazilian educator and a second reader.
